## 🔒 Aisle Security Analysis

We found **3** potential security issue(s) in this PR:

| # | Severity | Title |
|---|----------|-------|
| 1 | 🟠 High | CPU/memory DoS via quadratic truncation loop and unbounded file reads in memory_get excerpt building |
| 2 | 🟡 Medium | Tool-result size cap bypass via multi-block truncation allowing aggregate output > maxChars |
| 3 | 🟡 Medium | Per-agent skill allowlist can default to "main" when agentId is undefined |



<details>
<summary>1. 🟠 CPU/memory DoS via quadratic truncation loop and unbounded file reads in memory_get excerpt building</summary>

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Severity** | High |
| **CWE** | [CWE-400](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html) |
| **Location** | `src/memory-host-sdk/host/read-file-shared.ts:32-37` |

#### Description

The new bounded read helpers can be abused to consume excessive CPU and memory when processing large files or very large `lines` requests.

- `fitLinesToCharBudget()` repeatedly rebuilds the output string using `slice(...).join("\n")` while decrementing `includedLines` one-by-one until the string fits `maxChars`. For large `lines` windows this becomes **O(n²)** string work (many joins of large arrays).
- `buildMemoryReadResult()` splits the entire file content into an array (`content.split("\n")`) regardless of the requested excerpt size.
- Callers (`readMemoryFile`) read the full file into memory (`fs.readFile(absPath, "utf-8")`) with **no file size cap**.

If an attacker can cause `memory_get` (or any path using these helpers) to read a very large `.md` file and/or request a very large `lines` window, the process can experience CPU spikes, long event-loop stalls, and high memory usage (DoS).

#### Recommendation

Implement truncation in **linear time** and enforce **hard caps** on both input size and file size.

1) Replace the decrement-and-join loop with a single pass that accumulates until the char budget is reached:

```ts
function fitLinesToCharBudget({ lines, maxChars }: { lines: string[]; maxChars: number }) {
  let used = 0;
  let included = 0;
  const out: string[] = [];

  for (const line of lines) {
    const extra = (included === 0 ? 0 : 1) + line.length; // +1 for newline
    if (included > 0 && used + extra > maxChars) break;
    if (included === 0 && line.length > maxChars) {
      return { text: line.slice(0, maxChars), includedLines: 1, hardTruncatedSingleLine: true };
    }
    out.push(line);
    used += extra;
    included++;
  }

  return { text: out.join("\n"), includedLines: included, hardTruncatedSingleLine: false };
}
```

2) Enforce maximum `lines` in the `memory_get` schema/validation (e.g. `lines <= 5000`) and clamp any higher value server-side.

3) Before `fs.readFile`, reject files above a safe size limit (e.g. `stat.size <= 1–5MB`) or implement streaming reads that only read the required slice.

</details>

<details>
<summary>2. 🟡 Tool-result size cap bypass via multi-block truncation allowing aggregate output > maxChars</summary>

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **CWE** | [CWE-400](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html) |
| **Location** | `src/agents/pi-embedded-runner/tool-result-truncation.ts:272-299` |

#### Description

`truncateToolResultMessage()` is intended to cap tool result text to `maxChars` (used as a hard limit before persistence). However, it allocates a *per-text-block* budget and truncates each block independently. With many text blocks, the sum of per-block budgets can exceed `maxChars`, so the resulting message can remain far larger than the configured cap.

Impact:
- A tool (or plugin hook that rewrites tool results) can emit a toolResult `content` array with many `{type:"text"}` blocks.
- Even after truncation runs, each block may retain up to `minKeepChars + suffix` (clamped to `<= maxChars` *per block*), so total retained text can be roughly `N * minKeepChars`.
- This defeats the “hard size cap before persistence” in `session-tool-result-guard.ts`, enabling context-window DoS / prompt flooding via oversized persisted tool results.

Vulnerable code (per-block budgeting without enforcing a global aggregate cap):
```ts
const blockBudget = Math.max(
  1,
  Math.min(maxChars, Math.max(minKeepChars + defaultSuffix.length, proportionalBudget)),
);
...
text: truncateToolResultText(textBlock.text, blockBudget, ...)
```


#### Recommendation

Enforce the cap at the **message level**, not only per block.

Options:
1) **Global budgeting pass**: allocate budgets so that the sum of all block budgets is `<= maxChars`, then truncate each block to its allocated budget.
2) **Post-truncation verification**: after truncating blocks, recompute total length and further truncate (e.g., reduce from the largest blocks first) until `getToolResultTextLength(result) <= maxChars`.
3) **Hard cap number of text blocks**: if there are too many text blocks, merge them or replace excess blocks with a single truncation notice.

Example approach (2):
```ts
let truncated = { ...msg, content: newContent } as AgentMessage;
let total = getToolResultTextLength(truncated);
while (total > maxChars) {
  ​// reduce budgets further (e.g., truncate last/longest text block) until total <= maxChars
  ​// ensure suffix handling still respects maxChars
  total = getToolResultTextLength(truncated);
}
return truncated;
```

Also consider applying `minKeepChars` only when it is feasible under an aggregate cap (e.g., `minKeepChars <= Math.floor(maxChars / textBlockCount)`).

</details>

<details>
<summary>3. 🟡 Per-agent skill allowlist can default to "main" when agentId is undefined</summary>

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **CWE** | [CWE-284](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/284.html) |
| **Location** | `src/agents/skills/agent-filter.ts:9-18` |

#### Description

`resolveAgentEntry()` normalizes a possibly-undefined `agentId` using `normalizeAgentId()`, which maps empty/undefined to `DEFAULT_AGENT_ID` ("main"). As a result, callers that omit `agentId` may unintentionally resolve the **"main"** agent entry and apply its `skills` allowlist/overrides instead of falling back to shared defaults.

This creates a broken access-control condition where:

- **input**: `agentId` is `undefined` (e.g., optional call sites)
- **normalization**: `normalizeAgentId(undefined)` becomes `"main"`
- **policy lookup**: config entry for agent `id: "main"` is selected
- **impact**: effective skill filter may become **more permissive** than intended defaults (or different than expected), potentially widening the set of skills/tools exposed.

Vulnerable code:
```ts
const normalizedAgentId = normalizeAgentId(agentId);
return cfg.agents?.list?.find((entry) => normalizeAgentId(entry.id) === normalizedAgentId);
```

#### Recommendation

Do not resolve a specific agent entry when `agentId` is missing/undefined; instead, fall back to defaults.

Suggested fix (guard before normalization):
```ts
function resolveAgentEntry(cfg: OpenClawConfig | undefined, agentId: string | undefined) {
  if (!cfg || !agentId) return undefined;
  const normalizedAgentId = normalizeAgentId(agentId);
  return cfg.agents?.list?.find((entry) => normalizeAgentId(entry.id) === normalizedAgentId);
}

export function resolveEffectiveAgentSkillFilter(cfg: OpenClawConfig | undefined, agentId: string | undefined) {
  if (!cfg) return undefined;
  const agentEntry = agentId ? resolveAgentEntry(cfg, agentId) : undefined;
  if (agentEntry && Object.hasOwn(agentEntry, "skills")) {
    return normalizeSkillFilter(agentEntry.skills);
  }
  return normalizeSkillFilter(cfg.agents?.defaults?.skills);
}
```

This ensures missing agent identity cannot implicitly select `main`'s policy.

</details>


