## 🔒 Aisle Security Analysis

We found **2** potential security issue(s) in this PR:

| # | Severity | Title |
|---|----------|-------|
| 1 | 🟡 Medium | Algorithmic complexity DoS risk in global exec.finished deduplication Map pruning |
| 2 | 🟡 Medium | Deduplication key collision for exec.finished events due to unescaped delimiter in fingerprint |



<details>
<summary>1. 🟡 Algorithmic complexity DoS risk in global exec.finished deduplication Map pruning</summary>

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **CWE** | [CWE-400](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html) |
| **Location** | `src/gateway/server-node-events.ts:122-153` |

#### Description

The new `recentExecFinishedRuns` structure is a **global per-process** `Map` used to dedupe `exec.finished` events. When the map exceeds `MAX_RECENT_EXEC_FINISHED_RUNS` (2000), the pruning logic can require a **full scan of the map on every insertion** in the worst case:

- Input: `runId` and `sessionKey` are derived from node-supplied event payloads (`exec.finished`). A malicious/compromised node can emit many `exec.finished` events with **unique `runId`s**.
- State: the dedupe map is global (module-level), so one noisy node/tenant can create CPU overhead and eviction affecting other sessions handled by the same gateway process.
- Sink: once `size > 2000`, the code iterates `for (const [key, ts] of recentExecFinishedRuns)` until it can reduce size (or finishes scanning). If all entries are within the 10-minute window, **no deletions occur in the `for` loop**, so it scans ~2000 entries *per incoming event*.

Vulnerable code:
```ts
recentExecFinishedRuns.set(fingerprint, params.now);
if (recentExecFinishedRuns.size > MAX_RECENT_EXEC_FINISHED_RUNS) {
  const cutoff = params.now - EXEC_FINISHED_RUN_DEDUPE_WINDOW_MS;
  for (const [key, ts] of recentExecFinishedRuns) {
    if (ts < cutoff) {
      recentExecFinishedRuns.delete(key);
    }
    if (recentExecFinishedRuns.size <= MAX_RECENT_EXEC_FINISHED_RUNS) {
      break;
    }
  }
  while (recentExecFinishedRuns.size > MAX_RECENT_EXEC_FINISHED_RUNS) {
    const oldestKey = recentExecFinishedRuns.keys().next().value;
    ...
    recentExecFinishedRuns.delete(oldestKey);
  }
}
```

This creates an **algorithmic CPU amplification** vector: after the map is full and within-window, every new unique runId causes an O(n) scan (n≈2000). At high event rates this can cause sustained CPU usage and degraded service (DoS).

#### Recommendation

Avoid per-event full-map scans and reduce cross-tenant blast radius.

**Options (pick one or combine):**

1) **Make pruning O(1) amortized** by relying only on insertion order for eviction (no scan): since the map is insertion-ordered, delete oldest entries directly to enforce the cap, and accept that some entries may be <10 minutes old.

```ts
recentExecFinishedRuns.set(fingerprint, params.now);
while (recentExecFinishedRuns.size > MAX_RECENT_EXEC_FINISHED_RUNS) {
  const oldestKey = recentExecFinishedRuns.keys().next().value;
  if (oldestKey === undefined) break;
  recentExecFinishedRuns.delete(oldestKey);
}
```

2) **Use a queue + map** (or an LRU cache library) to track timestamps and prune expired entries incrementally without scanning.

3) **Shard state** by sessionKey and/or nodeId (e.g., `Map<sessionKey, LRU>`), so one noisy tenant/node cannot force work/evictions for others.

4) Add **rate limiting** / authentication for node events to prevent a node from flooding `exec.finished` with unique runIds.

</details>

<details>
<summary>2. 🟡 Deduplication key collision for exec.finished events due to unescaped delimiter in fingerprint</summary>

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **Severity** | Medium |
| **CWE** | [CWE-20](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html) |
| **Location** | `src/gateway/server-node-events.ts:122-137` |

#### Description

`shouldDropDuplicateExecFinished` builds a Map key by concatenating `sessionKey` and `runId` using the delimiter `"::"`:

- If `sessionKey` or `runId` can contain `"::"`, **different (sessionKey, runId) pairs can produce the same fingerprint**, e.g. `("a", "b::c")` and `("a::b", "c")` both yield `"a::b::c"`.
- A collision will cause the gateway to incorrectly treat a legitimate `exec.finished` as a duplicate and drop it within the 10-minute dedupe window.
- Because `runId` can be provided by the caller (`normalizeOptionalString(opts.params.runId) ?? crypto.randomUUID()` in `src/node-host/invoke-system-run.ts`), an attacker who can influence `runId` (or who can send node events) may be able to trigger **cross-run/session suppression** (denial of service / loss of auditability for exec results).

Vulnerable code:
```ts
const fingerprint = `${params.sessionKey}::${params.runId}`;
const previousTs = recentExecFinishedRuns.get(fingerprint);
```


#### Recommendation

Avoid ambiguous string concatenation for composite keys.

Safer options:

1) Use a nested map keyed by the components:
```ts
const recentExecFinishedRuns = new Map<string, Map<string, number>>();

function shouldDropDuplicateExecFinished({ sessionKey, runId, now }: {sessionKey: string; runId: string; now: number}) {
  const byRun = recentExecFinishedRuns.get(sessionKey) ?? new Map<string, number>();
  recentExecFinishedRuns.set(sessionKey, byRun);
  const previousTs = byRun.get(runId);
  if (typeof previousTs === 'number' && now - previousTs <= EXEC_FINISHED_RUN_DEDUPE_WINDOW_MS) return true;
  byRun.set(runId, now);
  return false;
}
```

2) If a single string key is required, encode components unambiguously (e.g., JSON):
```ts
const fingerprint = JSON.stringify([sessionKey, runId]);
```

Additionally, consider enforcing a reasonable maximum length/character set for `runId` at the boundary where it is accepted from callers to reduce DoS risk from extremely large keys.

</details>


